WEBVTT
TITLE Sliding Window, Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat: Windows, Sequence Numbers and Utilisation — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A stop-and-wait sender puts one frame on the wire, then stops and waits for its acknowledgement.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Transmission takes Tt = 8 ms; the round trip takes 2 Tp = 20 ms; the cycle is 28 ms for one frame.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Efficiency is useful time over cycle time: 8 over 28 is 0.2857, so 28.57 per cent of the link.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Twenty of the twenty-eight milliseconds are a pipe with nothing in it. The cure is to keep more frames in that pipe.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A window of W frames may be outstanding at once. The receiver holds a window too, and it is the receiver window that separates the protocols.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
An acknowledgement slides the window by one; the frames inside it travel while the sender keeps transmitting.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
W frames per cycle of one plus two a gives W over one plus two a, capped at one.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
On our link a is 1.25, so one plus two a is 3.5: windows 1, 2, 3 give 0.286, 0.571, 0.857, and 4 saturates the link.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
GATE CSE 2025 Set 2, Q36: 3000-bit frames at 2000 bits per second, one-way delay 100 ms. Utilisation?

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Tt is 1500 ms and 2 Tp is 200 ms, so the cycle is 1700 ms and efficiency is 1500 over 1700, which is 88.24 per cent.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
GATE CSE 2023, Q7 asks which scenario gives the LOWEST utilisation. Written out, efficiency is L times v over L times v plus two d B.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Longer link and higher transmission rate. Both changes enlarge the idle stretch, and neither changes the frame.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
GATE CSE 2026 Set 1, Q45: 1000-bit frames, 100 kbps, one-way delay 100 ms. What is the smallest window for 100 per cent utilisation?

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Tt is 10 ms, Tp is 100 ms, so a is 10 and one plus two a is 21. The window needed is 21 frames.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
When one plus two a is not a whole number, round up. A window of 3.5 frames does not exist.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The window is the bandwidth-delay product: the data that must be in flight to keep the link busy.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Go-Back-N: the receiver accepts only the next expected frame and acknowledges cumulatively.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Frame 2 is lost. Frames 3 and 4 arrive, are discarded, and the sender times out on frame 2.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Twelve frames with frame 2 lost once: window 4 costs 17 transmissions, window 8 costs 21.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
What Go-Back-N saves is memory: one buffer at the receiver, one timer at the sender, and no reordering.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Selective Repeat gives the receiver a window the same width as the sender and buffers out-of-order frames.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Frame 2 is lost, but 3 and 4 are buffered and acknowledged. When 2 is resent, all three are delivered.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The same twelve frames with the same loss cost 13 transmissions at either window size.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The price is buffering: W frames at each end, twice the sequence space, and reordering logic.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Three sequence bits give eight numbers, 0 to 7. Can a window of 8 be safe?

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Send 0 to 7, have them all delivered and acknowledged, send the next eight reusing 0 to 7, and then an old duplicate acknowledgement arrives.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
So one number must be held back: the window is at most two-to-the-m minus one, which is 7 here.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Computed for m equal to 2, 3, 4 and 5, the first window that fails is 4, 8, 16 and 32 — exactly two-to-the-m.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Selective Repeat buffers, so its receiver window is as wide as the sender window. That is what costs it the sequence space.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
With three bits and a window of 5: deliver 0 to 4, lose every acknowledgement, and the receiver window becomes 5, 6, 7, 0, 1.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
A window of 4 is safe, because the receiver window becomes 4, 5, 6, 7 and stops short of 0.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
So the bound is two-to-the-m minus one: 2, 4, 8, 16 for two, three, four and five bits, and the first failing window is one more.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
One loss, twelve frames: Go-Back-N transmits 17 at window 4 and 21 at window 8; Selective Repeat transmits 13 at both.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Elapsed time differs too: 24 against 23 at window 4, and 24 against 20 at window 8, in transmission times.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The window you need is still the bandwidth-delay product, and it decides whether the sequence field is wide enough.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Choose by what the link is like: clean links make the two identical, lossy links favour Selective Repeat, memory-poor receivers favour Go-Back-N.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Four lines: Tt, then Tp, then a and one plus two a, then substitute.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Trap one: doubling the one-way delay twice. Trap two: bytes against bits. Trap three: forgetting the plus one.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Trap four: giving Go-Back-N the Selective Repeat bound. Trap five: assuming a bigger window always transmits less.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Write down which quantity the question wants — efficiency, window, bits or transmissions — and the five traps cannot survive.

